    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d661">

                <pb facs="0952" n="942" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg942"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Colonel James C. Davis to General Marshall </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-02-17">17 February
                        1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>97562. No agreement of any kind has been signed by Eximbank and Chinese covering
                    cotton loan. Reference your 200 dated 16 February. Only action here has been
                    approval of extension of credit for purpose <pb facs="0953" n="943"
                        xml:id="pg943"/> of purchasing cotton. In approving extension of the credit
                    the Board of Directors of Eximbank provided that before the credit should be
                    available to Chinese the Chinese would have to agree to certain undertakings
                    with regard to the manner in which the funds would be used. Situation in this
                    respect is as stated in State Deptel 236 of 6 February and State Deptel 297
                    dated 15 February. Eximbank position does not constitute unilateral action with
                    respect to a mutually agreed covenant but rather states the terms upon which the
                    bank is willing to enter into an agreement to extend the desired credit. As
                    stated in State Deptel 297 dated 15 February Eximbank desires your comment on
                    its position.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
